cide does not stand up to scrutiny  by any honest and clear-sighted  historian  first no matter who discovered the new  world it's inevitable that a large  proportion of new world inhabitants  would have died within the first few  decades after first contact  this is universally acknowledged by  specialists in the field  the new world population was smaller and  more homogeneous than the old world  population  so its people had less immunity to  disease than the people of the old world  where disease communities from africa  asia and europe had been  intermingling for millennia even though  some european captains did try to spread  smallpox around a few forts and villages  from time to time  the effect of their efforts was almost  negligible  compared with the natural spread of  disease old world rice and wheat  agriculture could support  high population densities per hectare  and the huge disparity in population  density between the old world and the  new  therefore made it likely that excess  population was going to flow from the  old world to the new  furthermore as soon as old world  colonists began to set up the farming  and city-dwelling societies which they  imported from home  population growth in the colonies was  bound to outstrip  that of native american groups low  population devastated by disease is  therefore the main explanation for why  british north america was so thoroughly  europeanized when many other parts of  the globe were not  in fact the europeans were surprisingly  ungenocidal  given their clear technological  advantages over the rest of the world  for a period of several centuries  the truth is that few other  civilizations given similar power over  so much of the world's people would have  behaved in a less reprehensible manner  this is not to give europeans a pat on  the back  rather it is to point out that europeans  are regularly painted  as the very worst society on earth when  in fact they had  the power to do far far more evil than 